in 1976  a deadly illness erupted in a remote  province of zaire  belgian nuns tending to the sick  described horrific symptoms  followed by agonizing deaths it took tax  tissue around the body  uh basically attacks everything in the  human body except for your bones  doctors from zaire europe and america  traveled to the epicenter to investigate  when responders got there they had no  idea what they were looking at but they  did know  that they were looking at this deadly  new virus  the researchers might not have known  what this new virus was  but they learned how it spread so  quickly  medical supplies were so scarce here  that the belgian nurses had resorted to  reusing hypodermic needles  this virus had an astounding mortality  rate  of nearly 90 percent  in less than two months it claimed some  300 lives  then suddenly the nightmare ended and no  one knew why  they just knew the virus popped out of  nowhere and it disappeared just as  quickly  epidemiologists named it ebola zaire  after the nearby ebola river  and dreaded its return which it did  in just a few relatively contained  outbreaks over the next decade  but the greatest fear was that someday  the virus would emerge in a densely  populated region  which it also did  so back in 1989 in the washington suburb  of reston  all of a sudden a bunch of monkeys at a  warehouse started dying  hazelton laboratories rest in virginia  one of the largest  animal testing companies in the country  a shipment of 100 long-tailed macaques  came in from the philippines  two were dead on arrival  [Music]  the illness quickly spread to dozens of  monkeys  desperate for a diagnosis the company  sent  samples to a high security lab run by  the military  the us army medical research institute  of infectious diseases  or usamrid lieutenant colonel nancy  jacks  examined the tissues what we found then  was really pretty alarming  i was seeing a section of liver  i saw all these very large cells which  are called macrophages  and they were full of material  which is very typical in a filo virus  infection  a filo virus a tiny spaghetti-like  strand  that replicates at lightning speed and  causes devastating diseases  dr jacks was very familiar with them she  worked behind layers of bomb resistant  walls  where the army stored an archive of  frozen killers  marburg ebola sudan  and the most lethal of all ebola zaire  dr jacks used her stash of filo viruses  to compare to the monkey sample  and to her surprise the one that matched  was the virus that struck zaire in 1976  we're thinking well that's not possible  we need to check it again  and they checked it three times and  every time there was no mix-up it was  showing positive for ebola as i hear  i think nancy came to my office  you're not going to believe what just  happened you know it looks like we've  got  ebola virus in these monkeys in virginia  there was a lot of confusion and going  oh wow this is  this is really crazy what are we going  to do we immediately had to kick into  high gear  the army formed the battle plan first  order  euthanized the monkeys to prevent an  epidemic  nancy's husband colonel jerry jacks led  the team  nobody likes killing monkeys but we were  in an emergency situation it was  something that we had to do  to make sure that we didn't have a  significant outbreak within the human  population  they dodged a bullet and they soon  realized  this strain of ebola wasn't harmful to  humans  but they knew it was only a matter of  time before they'd have to face the  unimaginable  the question of course is are you going  to get a mutation that  then makes it more airborne or makes it  more  contagious what we did in a contained  building  was pretty dramatic but imagine if it  was a city  the nightmare scenario for global public  health officials is something that  combines  the transmissibility of something like  the spanish flu which infected  probably about a billion people a  century ago and the mortality of ebola  what keeps me up at night is that we'll  have a disease that's transmitted by  droplets something that is respiratory  borne  that is a virus like influenza likely  influenza  that has both high degree of of uh  transmissibility and high degree of  morbidity and mortality  if something like that spreads around  the world we're just not ready to fight  it  [Music]  you

